WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 293: William the Conqueror: The Norman Conquest, Hastings, and the Domesday Book

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:18.960
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the Ironwilled Duke of Normandy, whose invasion in 1066 permanently altered the language, architecture, and destiny of Britain.

4
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of William the Conqueror.

5
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:36.720
[George] Born around 1028 in the Castle of Falaise in Normandy, William was the illegitimate son of Duke Robert the Magnificent and Hurleva, the beautiful daughter of a local tanner.

6
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:48.240
[George] Taunted throughout his youth by noble rivals as William the Bastard, seven-year-old William inherited the Duchy of Normandy in 1035 when his father died on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem.

7
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.360
[George] For twelve perilous years, Normandy descended into anarchic feudal bloodshed.

8
00:00:53.360 --> 00:01:04.640
[George] Assassination plots swirled around the boy Duke, his guardians and chamberlains were murdered in his bedroom, and young William was forced to flee through moonlit forests to escape rebel barons.

9
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:21.680
[George] In 1047, aided by King Henry I of France, 19-year-old William crushed the rebel Norman nobility at the Battle of Vals Dunes, asserting absolute feudal control over his duchy through iron discipline, swift justice, and an uncompromising martial will.

10
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:37.760
[George] His maternal grandfather Fulbert was a skinner and tanner whose foul-smelling trade in animal skins and urine was treated with aristocratic disgust by the proud Norman nobility, who mocked young William's origins by hanging wet cowhides from castle battlements during sieges.

11
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.360
[George] William responded with cold, terrifying brutality.

12
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:52.400
[George] When the garrison of Alenson beat cowhides on their ramparts, William stormed the castle, severed the hands and feet of thirty-two defenders, and hurled their bleeding limbs over the walls.

13
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:58.400
[George] Demonstrating an uncompromising ruthlessness that cowed rebel barons into absolute obedience.

14
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:12.280
[George] Under William's iron hand, Normandy was transformed into the most disciplined and militarily efficient feudal state in Western Europe, featuring heavily armored shock cavalry trained to charge in cohesive Kushtland's formations.

15
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:23.640
[George] In 1064, fate intervened when Earl Harold Godwinson, England's foremost Saxon noble, was shipwrecked on the coast of Pontheu and brought to William's court in Rouen.

16
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:41.720
[George] In a celebrated episode depicted on the Bayou Tapestry, William treated Harold with chivalric honor, took him on campaign in Brittany, and compelled him to swear a solemn oath over hidden sacred saints' relics, pledging to support William's claim to the English crown upon Edward the Confessor's death.

17
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:49.960
[George] When King Edward died in January 1066 and Harold accepted the English crown, William erupted in righteous fury.

18
00:02:49.960 --> 00:03:00.360
[George] Securing the blessing of Pope Alexander I I and a papal banner, William mobilized an invasion fleet of seven hundred flat-bottomed transport ships at Divy's sur Mur.

19
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:09.160
[George] Gathering seven thousand Norman, Breton and Flemish knights, heavy cavalry, and archers to enforce his claim by holy right of sword.

20
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:16.760
[George] The fateful oath sworn by Earl Harold at Bayou was the centerpiece of William's legal and moral justification for invasion.

21
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:27.400
[George] By swearing over holy reliquaries containing the bones of ancient Norman saints, Harold had bound his immortal soul under medieval canon law to support William's succession.

22
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:39.560
[George] When Harold accepted the crown from the Saxon Witon, William astutely framed the impending war not as a conquest of plunder, but as a holy crusade to punish an oathbreaking perjurer and usurper.

23
00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.280
[George] Papal endorsement from Rome galvanized knights from across northern France.

24
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.560
[George] Flanders and Brittany, who gathered at the mouth of the Somme River at St.

25
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:58.880
[George] Valerie, where William organized an astonishing logistical feat, assembling hundreds of tons of iron chainmill, thousands of warhorses, and millions of arrows.

26
00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:10.320
[George] Trapped on the Norman coast for six grueling weeks by adverse northerly gales, William's fleet finally set sail across the English Channel aboard his magnificent flagship, the Mora.

27
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:17.040
[George] A gift from his wife Matilda of Flanders bearing a gilded bronze boy blowing an ivory trumpet at its prow.

28
00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:25.120
[George] On September 28, 1066, the Norman armada made an unopposed amphibious landing at Pevensey Bay in Sussex.

29
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:33.920
[George] As William stepped onto the English gravel, he tripped and fell flat on his face, a terrifying omen of ill fortune to his superstitious knights.

30
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:40.400
[George] Rising instantly with both hands clutching fists full of English sand, the Duke roared with lightning wit.

31
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.960
[George] By the splendor of God, I have seized England with my two hands.

32
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:57.760
[George] Marching east to Hastings, William erected a prefabricated wooden castle, deliberately burning and plundering the Sussex estates of the House of Godwin to bait Harold into an immediate, hasty, decisive confrontation.

33
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:08.480
[George] The physical ordeal of transporting thousands of spirited warhorses across the choppy waters of the English Channel in low sided wooden sailing transports was an unprecedented gamble.

34
00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.320
[George] Horses had to be strapped into canvas slings to prevent panic.

35
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.280
[George] And any gale could have foundered the entire fleet.

36
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] When the Norman army landed at Pevensey, finding the Roman fortress walls of Anderitam abandoned because King Harold was seven days' forced march away in Yorkshire fighting Harold Hardrata at Stamford Bridge, William immediately secured his beachhead with defensive palisades.

37
00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:39.480
[George] William understood that time was on Harold's side, leading the Duke to launch a deliberate campaign of psychological warfare.

38
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:49.480
[George] Ravaging the Sussex towns and coastal villages to provoke Harold's furious pride and compel the exhausted Saxon king to rush south without waiting for northern levies.

39
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:59.480
[George] On Saturday, October 14, 1066, on Senlac Hill, seven miles north of Hastings, two distinct military doctrines collided.

40
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:12.840
[George] King Harold's 7,000 Saxon housecarls and Thanes formed an impenetrable, three-foot-thick shield wall atop the ridge, wielding brutal two-handed Danish battle axes that severed a horse and rider with a single blow.

41
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] For six agonizing hours, Norman cavalry and infantry broke against the Saxon wall like waves against granite.

42
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:26.520
[George] When rumors swept the ranks that William had been slain, the Norman line began a panicked route down the hill.

43
00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:34.120
[George] Tearing off his iron helmet to expose his face, William rode before his fleeing troops, roaring, Look at me.

44
00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.480
[George] I live, and by God's grace I will conquer.

45
00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:47.000
[George] William ordered a series of feigned retreats, luring undisciplined Saxon militia down the hill into open ground where Norman heavy cavalry wheeled and slaughtered them.

46
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:56.680
[George] As the autumn dusk fell, an arrow pierced Harold's eye, Saxon resistance collapsed, and King Harold fell hacked to pieces beneath his dragon standard.

47
00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.880
[George] The clash at Senlac Hill was a grueling, A-tower trial of human endurance.

48
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:13.480
[George] The Saxon housecarls, standing shoulder to shoulder with round shields locked rim over rim, formed an impregnable wall that repelled repeated charges of Norman heavy horse and crossbow fire.

49
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:27.120
[George] The decisive turning point came late in the afternoon when William, having halted a disastrous panic among his left-wing Bretons, systematically deployed the feigned retreat, ordering his cavalry to simulate terrified flight.

50
00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:36.720
[George] The undisciplined Saxon militia broke ranks and surged down the hill in pursuit, only to be encircled, cut off, and hacked to pieces on the muddy slopes.

51
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:47.600
[George] When a falling arrow struck Harold near his eye and Norman knights broke through the thinned Saxon center to mutilate the fallen king, the Anglo-Saxon monarchy was extinguished in blood.

52
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:59.360
[George] Marching in a wide arc around London, ravaging Berkshire, Hertfordshire, and Kent, William isolated the capital until the Saxon Witon and Archbishop Eldred surrendered at Berkhamstead.

53
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:08.480
[George] On Christmas Day 1066, inside Edward the Confessor's newly consecrated Westminster Abbey, William was crowned King of England.

54
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.600
[George] The ceremony was marred by terrifying chaos.

55
00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:25.360
[George] When Archbishop Eldred asked the assembled Saxons in English if they accepted William as king and Bishop Geoffrey of Countences asked the Normans in French, the congregation shouted Yay, yea, in thunderous acclamation.

56
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:36.480
[George] Mistaking the loud shouts inside the stone church for an armed rebellion, Norman cavalry stationed outside panicked and began setting fire to the surrounding Saxon houses and shops.

57
00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:52.240
[George] As smoke billowed into the dark nave and the terrified congregation fled the burning abbey, thirty-eight-year-old William knelt trembling before the altar, anointed with holy chrism by a handful of shivering priests, crowned sovereign of a conquered and burning realm.

58
00:08:52.240 --> 00:09:03.320
[George] The horrific coronation scene on Christmas Day 1066 encapsulated the violent, tragic chasm separating the Norman conquerors from their conquered Saxon subjects.

59
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:15.160
[George] The sound of fire crackling through nearby thatched roofs and the screams of fleeing citizens echoed off the soaring Romanesque stone vaults as Archbishop Eildred hastily placed the crown on William's head.

60
00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.000
[George] William recognized that London was the economic heart of the realm.

61
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:36.760
[George] He granted the city a royal charter in old English confirming their ancient mercantile customs, yet simultaneously broke ground on the Tower of London to maintain an unblinking military garrison within striking distance of the city gates, proving that the Norman crown ruled by force of arms.

62
00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:47.480
[George] Saxon resistance erupted into bloody regional revolts, culminating in the winter of 1069 when Danish fleets joined Saxon rebels to sack the Norman garrison at York.

63
00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:53.800
[George] William's vengeance was apocalyptic, in the systematic atrocity remembered as the Harrying of the North.

64
00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:05.400
[George] William split his cavalry into roving death squads across Yorkshire and Durham, burning every village, slaughtering livestock, salting crop fields, and destroying farming implements.

65
00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:13.720
[George] Over one hundred thousand peasants perished from exposure and famine, with refugees forced to eat horses, dogs and human flesh.

66
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:20.520
[George] Sixteen years later, the doomsday books still recorded vast swathes of northern shires as barren waste.

67
00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:36.840
[George] To hold the traumatized kingdom in permanent subjugation, William erected hundreds of stone mot and Bailey fortresses across England, culminating in the colossal White Tower of London, a soaring fortress of white con limestone designed to overaw the Saxon citizenry.

68
00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:54.640
[George] The systematic devastation of Yorkshire in the winter of 1069 was an act of calculated military genocide that shocked even hardened contemporary chroniclers like Orderic Vitalis, who wrote that he could find no words to praise a king who ordered the starvation of innocent women and children.

69
00:10:54.640 --> 00:11:08.640
[George] William spent Christmas 1069 marching his troops across the snow-covered Pennine moors, his cavalry destroying food stores and burning farmsteads until no living thing was left for a hundred miles between the Humber and the Tees.

70
00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:27.680
[George] The stone castles William erected across every major shire town, including Warwick, Nottingham, York and Lincoln, transformed the landscape of England into an occupied military fortress, replacing Saxon wooden halls with soaring limestone keeps that symbolized absolute Norman domination.

71
00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:41.680
[George] At Gloucester at Christmas 1085, William held deep speech with his royal council, ordering an unprecedented administrative survey of every height of land, mill, fishpond, ox, and pig across his realm.

72
00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:57.680
[George] Completed in 1086 by royal commissioners, the Doomsday Book, so named because its judgments were as irrevocable as the day of doom, systematically recorded the total transfer of wealth from Saxon Thanes to barely two hundred Norman barons and bishops.

73
00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:10.800
[George] In August 1087, while campaigning against the French king at Mance, William's horse reared on burning embers, throwing the corpulent king violently against his iron saddle pommel and rupturing his bowels.

74
00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.520
[George] Dying in agonizing torment at the Priory of St.

75
00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:23.360
[George] Gervis near Rouen on September 9, 1087, at age 59, William confessed his sins, lamenting the bloody conquest of England.

76
00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:33.240
[George] William the Conqueror had forged an enduring Anglo-Norman state, marrying Viking fire to French chivalry and creating the architecture of the modern English realm.

77
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:39.080
[George] The Doomsday Survey was the most sophisticated administrative achievement of the medieval world.

78
00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:52.440
[George] Royal commissioners held sworn inquisitions in every shire, interrogating local priests, reeves, and juries to record who owned every acre of arable land under Edward the Confessor and who held it under King William.

79
00:12:52.440 --> 00:13:04.760
[George] By 1086, less than 5% of English land remained in the hands of native Saxons, as an entire ruling class was dispossessed and replaced by a foreign French-speaking aristocracy.

80
00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:12.760
[George] Dying in Normandy in 1087, his corpulent body so swollen that it burst when shoved into its stone sarcophagus at St.

81
00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:17.080
[George] Atian in Cannes, William the Conqueror left an immortal legacy.

82
00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:26.040
[George] He had severed England from the Scandinavian world and welded it irrevocably to the culture, law, and politics of continental Europe.

83
00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:47.160
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with his fiery, controversial son, King William Rufus and the fatal mystery of the Arrow in the New Forest.

84
00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.280
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

